# C4D ArchViz Workflow Tools

A collection of Python utilities for **Cinema 4D**, created to automate repetitive tasks and improve architectural visualization, GIS and scene optimization workflows.

These tools were originally developed to solve practical production problems encountered in architectural visualization projects.

The repository currently includes utilities for:

- Archicad / Cinema 4D scene optimization
- Material cleanup and conversion
- Object and hierarchy management
- QGIS / DXF / GIS workflows
- Parcel and topographic workflows
- D5 Render preparation

---

## Compatibility

Main development and testing environment:

```text
Cinema 4D 2023.1.2
Windows

Some scripts may also work with newer or older Cinema 4D versions, but they have not necessarily been tested.

Repository Structure
C4D-ArchViz-Workflow-Tools/
│
├── Archicad_Optimization/
│
├── Material_Conversion/
│
├── Material_Optimization/
│
├── Object_Management/
│
├── QGIS_Workflows/
│
├── LICENSE
└── README.md

Each tool has its own folder containing the Python script and, when necessary, specific instructions or an example Cinema 4D scene.

Archicad Optimization

Utilities designed to simplify and optimize Cinema 4D scenes imported from Archicad.

Examples include:

Connecting large numbers of imported objects
Cleaning imported hierarchies
Merging duplicate Null objects
Reducing unnecessary scene complexity

These tools are particularly useful when working with large architectural models containing hundreds or thousands of imported objects.

Material Conversion

Tools for recovering or converting materials from older workflows.

For example:

Legacy V-Ray Advanced Material Converter

Converts legacy V-Ray Advanced Materials from old Cinema 4D scenes into Cinema 4D Standard materials.

This tool was created primarily for recovering archived scenes made with older versions of V-Ray for Cinema 4D.

It is not intended as a converter for current V-Ray versions.

Material Optimization

Utilities for cleaning and simplifying material libraries.

Examples include:

Detecting duplicate Standard materials
Comparing material channels and texture assignments
Merging only materials with matching properties
Updating Texture Tags automatically

These tools can be useful for large architectural scenes where imported models generate many duplicated materials.

Object Management

General Cinema 4D workflow utilities.

Examples include:

Incremental object renaming
Creating instances from selected objects
Hierarchy management
Batch scene organization

These scripts are designed to reduce repetitive manual operations.

QGIS / GIS Workflows

Tools created for workflows involving:

QGIS
↓
DXF / GIS Data
↓
Cinema 4D
↓
D5 Render

This section contains utilities for working with parcel boundaries, cadastral information and topographic data.

Examples include:

Create Random Parcel Sweeps

Creates one Sweep object for every parcel spline using a template Sweep.

Rectangle heights can be randomized between user-defined values.

Useful for:

Parcel boundaries
Fences
Curbs
Urban visualization
Landscape visualization
Fix Parcel Spline Direction

Analyses closed parcel splines and ensures that their point direction is consistent.

This can be important when generating surfaces that will later be used as scatter zones.

One practical use case is preparing parcel surfaces for D5 Render Scatter, where inconsistent surface normals can cause vegetation to be generated in the wrong direction.

The script preserves the original X / Y / Z coordinates and only changes the point order when necessary.

Create Parcel Lofts

Creates one Loft object per parcel spline using a template Loft object.

This makes it possible to quickly transform large GIS parcel datasets into individual Cinema 4D surfaces.

These surfaces can then be used for:

Scatter zones
Vegetation distribution
Landscape studies
Urban planning visualization
Installation / Usage

These tools are standard Cinema 4D Python scripts.

They are not standalone applications.

A typical workflow is:

Download the desired .py script.
Open Cinema 4D.
Open the Cinema 4D Script Manager.
Load or paste the Python script.
Prepare the scene according to the instructions in the tool's README.
Run the script.

Some scripts require objects to be selected in a specific order.

Always read the README included with the tool before running it.

Example Scenes

Some tools include small Cinema 4D example scenes.

These scenes contain only simple geometry and are intended to demonstrate the expected hierarchy and workflow.

Example:

PARCEL_SPLINES
├── Parcel_001
├── Parcel_002
├── Parcel_003
└── Parcel_004

SWEEP_TEMPLATE
└── Rectangle

Example files are intentionally kept lightweight and independent from production projects.

Production Safety

Many scripts perform batch operations on objects, materials or hierarchies.

Before running a script on an important production scene:

Save your project first.

For large or critical scenes, it is recommended to test the script on a copy of the file.

Where possible, scripts include Cinema 4D Undo support, but saving the scene beforehand is still strongly recommended.

Why this repository exists

Architectural visualization projects often involve repetitive operations that are difficult or time-consuming to perform manually.

Typical examples include:

Cleaning large Archicad imports
Processing hundreds of GIS parcel splines
Fixing inconsistent imported geometry
Managing duplicated materials
Preparing geometry for D5 Render
Reorganizing complex Cinema 4D scenes

The goal of this repository is to turn these recurring production tasks into small, reusable tools.

Development

These tools are developed from real production workflow needs.

The scripts are created, tested and adjusted directly inside Cinema 4D, with AI-assisted development used as part of the scripting process.

The focus of the repository is not software engineering experimentation, but practical tools that can save time in architectural visualization workflows.

Contributions and Feedback

If you find a bug, have an improvement idea or use one of these tools in a different workflow, feel free to open an Issue.

Because Cinema 4D APIs can change between versions, mentioning your Cinema 4D version when reporting an issue is helpful.

Disclaimer

These scripts are provided as workflow utilities and may not cover every possible Cinema 4D scene configuration.

Always verify the result before using modified geometry or materials in production.

